## 27. Prime Alphabet Positions

Prime positions:

```text
2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23
```

Letters:

```text
B C E G K M Q S W
```

This may appear in advanced coding questions.

---

## 28. Composite Alphabet Positions

Composite positions from 1–26:

```text
4,6,8,9,10,12,14,15,16,18,20,21,22,24,25,26
```

Letters:

```text
D F H I J L N O P R T U V X Y Z
```

Remember:

```text
1 is neither prime nor composite.
```

---

## 29. Odd and Even Alphabet Positions

Odd:

```text
A C E G I K M O Q S U W Y
```

Even:

```text
B D F H J L N P R T V X Z
```

This can be used for binary-like coding:

```text
Odd  = 1
Even = 0
```

Example:

```text
ABC
```

positions:

```text
1,2,3
```

binary pattern:

```text
101
```

---

## 30. Binary-Style Letter Coding

A coding rule may be:

```text
Odd alphabet position = 1
Even alphabet position = 0
```

Example:

```text
CODE
```

positions:

```text
C = 3 → 1
O = 15 → 1
D = 4 → 0
E = 5 → 1
```

Therefore:

```text
CODE → 1101
```

---

## 31. Reverse Position Coding

Use:

```text
A = 26
B = 25
C = 24
...
Z = 1
```

Example:

```text
CAT
```

becomes:

```text
C = 24
A = 26
T = 7
```

Therefore:

```text
CAT → 24,26,7
```

---

## 32. Sum of Reverse Positions

For:

```text
CAT
```

reverse values:

```text
24 + 26 + 7 = 57
```

So:

```text
CAT → 57
```

---

## 33. Mirror Coding

Mirror coding uses:

```text
A ↔ Z
B ↔ Y
C ↔ X
...
```

Example:

```text
CAT
```

becomes:

```text
C → X
A → Z
T → G
```

Therefore:

```text
CAT → XZG
```

---

## 34. Mirror + Shift Coding

Some difficult questions combine operations.

Example:

```text
A → Z → A
B → Y → Z
C → X → Y
```

This could mean:

```text
mirror first
then +1
```

Always split a complicated transformation into stages:

```text
Original
  ↓
Mirror
  ↓
Shift
  ↓
Reverse
  ↓
Final code
```

---

## 35. Word Reversal Coding

Sometimes letters are simply reversed.

```text
APPLE → ELPPA
```

The transformation:

```text
ABCDE → EDCBA
```

Example:

```text
MANGO → OGNAM
```

---
